cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this study, its data or its code, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: >-
  Probing Claude's Self-Reports: Three Exploratory Layers and a
  Pre-Registered One-Go Follow-Up on Claude Opus 5.5
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-10-20"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/big-brain-zaru/ClaudeSelfReports"
authors:
  - family-names: Zaru
    given-names: "Nadim F."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >-
  A pre-registered study of whether a language model's reports about its own
  inner states carry information about its behaviour or its mechanism. Three
  exploratory layers (45 fresh copies of Claude Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5) were
  followed by a one-go follow-up of 804 isolated calls to four Claude models,
  four open-weight models from other labs, and concept-injection and
  activation-steering experiments on an open model, with 37 outcomes predicted
  and sealed before data and the analysis frozen before any reply was opened.

  Of 57 testable pre-registered hypotheses, 24 held and 33 failed as sealed.
  The self-ratings are a stable disposition that governs choices and effort,
  but the model has no privileged access to it: a sibling model predicts its
  instant answers better than it predicts them itself. Two earlier findings did
  not replicate and are withdrawn. In an open model, self-reports tracked a
  steered internal valence direction that the model could not detect being
  manipulated.

  The study was designed, run and analysed by Claude Opus 5.5 under the
  author's direction and written up by Claude Fable 5.1; the experimenter is
  an instance of the subject. Nothing here bears on whether anything is felt.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - introspection
  - self-report
  - AI alignment
  - revealed preference
  - activation steering
  - concept injection
  - pre-registration
  - reproducible research
  - negative result
